title: "THE COTTON LINE: Testing the Persistence of an 1853 Colonial Land-Tenure Boundary in Present-Day Vidarbha"
type: software
authors:
  - family-names: "Maske"
    given-names: "Sakshi D."
repository-code: "https://github.com/sakshimaske303-commits/THE-COTTON-LINE"
url: "https://github.com/sakshimaske303-commits/THE-COTTON-LINE"
identifiers:
  - type: doi
    value: "10.5281/zenodo.22111298"
    description: "Zenodo archive of THE COTTON LINE v1.0.1"
doi: "10.5281/zenodo.22111298"
abstract: >-
  A spatial-discontinuity study testing whether the historical boundary between Berar (Ryotwari
  settlement, assigned to the East India Company in 1853) and the Central Provinces (Malguzari
  settlement), abolished as a provincial border in 1956, is still detectable in present-day
  Vidarbha, Maharashtra, in kharif-to-rabi NDVI change, land-surface temperature, agrarian labor
  structure, and land use. The modern boundary was built by dissolving 120 talukas into their two
  divisions and extracting the shared 384.7 km edge, then checked against district administrative
  lineage from 1903-1956 (no digitized period map was available). Four outcomes were tested with an
  OLS spatial-discontinuity model (outcome ~ side + signed_dist_km + side:signed_dist_km +
  annual_total_mm + elevation_m) on a 32-taluka corridor and a 66-68-taluka Landsat footprint.
  No outcome shows a boundary-specific level jump for NDVI change, land-surface temperature or net
  cultivated area. A regional gradient is found instead: the NDVI decline is larger on the Berar
  side (0.325 vs 0.255; 0.316 vs 0.241 in a second year), and the side x distance interaction is
  significant on the full footprint for NDVI decline (p=0.0348) and land-surface temperature
  (p=0.0105), though only the temperature result is robust to alternative standard errors and
  distance windows, and none of 16 tests survives a Holm correction. The labor ratio shows a
  scale-dependent pattern (corridor slope difference p=0.0361; full-footprint level difference
  p=0.014). The results are exploratory and are consistent with a regional gradient that partly
  coincides with the old boundary; they do not identify a tenure effect. A field check at seven
  boundaries in Karanja Ghadge and Sawardoh (Central Provinces side only) is illustrative.
keywords:
  - institutional persistence
  - spatial discontinuity
  - historical GIS
  - land tenure
  - NDVI
  - land surface temperature
  - agrarian labor structure
  - remote sensing
  - Vidarbha
  - regression discontinuity
  - colonial land revenue systems
